<article class="env-lemma" id="05LR">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05LR">Lemma <span data-tag="05LR">15.19.2</span>.</a> In Situation <a href="/tag/05LP" data-tag="05LP">15.19.1</a> let $R' \to R''$ be an $R$-algebra map. Let $I' \subset R'$ and $I'R'' \subset I'' \subset R''$ be ideals. If (<a href="/tag/05LQ" data-tag="05LQ">15.19.1.1</a>) holds for $(R', I')$, then (<a href="/tag/05LQ" data-tag="05LQ">15.19.1.1</a>) holds for $(R'', I'')$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume (<a href="/tag/05LQ" data-tag="05LQ">15.19.1.1</a>) holds for $(R', I')$. Let $I''S'' + JS'' \subset \mathfrak q''$ be a prime of $S''$. Let $\mathfrak q' \subset S'$ be the corresponding prime of $S'$. Then both $I'S' \subset \mathfrak q'$ and $JS' \subset \mathfrak q'$ because the corresponding conditions hold for $\mathfrak q''$. Note that $(M'')_{\mathfrak q''}$ is a localization of the base change $M'_{\mathfrak q'} \otimes _ R R''$. Hence $(M'')_{\mathfrak q''}$ is flat over $R''$ as a localization of a flat module, see Algebra, Lemmas <a href="/tag/00HI" data-tag="00HI">10.39.7</a> and <a href="/tag/00HT" data-tag="00HT">10.39.18</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>